Station`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    119,\n    'civil',\n    'edit.pdf',\n    '  vcnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnxc',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-12',\n    '46',\n    'running',\n    NULL,\n    'Paid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    120,\n    'civil',\n    'Foormatii Gabaasa BQMHN kur 3ffaa kan Aanole itti guraamu.xlsx',\n    'jffffffffffffff',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-12',\n    '55',\n    'running',\n    NULL,\n    'Paid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    121,\n    'civil',\n    'formiii.docx',\n    'bnm',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-27',\n    '66',\n    'closed',\n    '<h2>COURT DECISION</h2><h2>GIRAAR JAARSOO DISTRICT COURT</h2><h3>FILE NO.: 121</h3><h3>DATE OF DECISION: __________</h3><h3>PLAINTIFFS</h3><ol><li>Mr. Indaalaa Charuu</li><li>Mrs. Zinnee Alamuu</li></ol><h3>DEFENDANT</h3><p>Mrs. Immabeet Indaalaa</p><h2>SUBJECT MATTER</h2><p>This case concerns a claim filed by the plaintiffs seeking reimbursement of construction expenses allegedly incurred in the construction of a residential house located in Burqaa Buukii Kebele, Fitche Town. The plaintiffs claim that they constructed the house using their own money, labor, and materials while the property was registered in the defendant’s name because she was their daughter and was underage at the time.</p><p>The plaintiffs requested payment of a total amount of ETB 177,674.32, representing expenses for construction materials, labor, roofing sheets, timber, nails, stone, mud plastering, and related construction costs.</p><p>The defendant denied the claim and argued that:</p><ol><li>The land and house are legally registered in her name.</li><li>She was married and capable of managing her own affairs during the construction period.</li><li>She and her husband contributed to the construction of the house.</li><li>The plaintiffs failed to produce sufficient documentary evidence proving the alleged expenses.</li><li>Some of the claims are barred by limitation due to the passage of time.</li></ol><h2>SUMMARY OF EVIDENCE</h2><h2>Plaintiffs’ Evidence</h2><p>The plaintiffs presented witness testimony stating that:</p><ul><li>The defendant was young when the land was obtained in her name.</li><li>The first plaintiff actively participated in the construction work.</li><li>The plaintiffs purchased roofing sheets, timber, nails, and other construction materials.</li><li>The plaintiffs provided labor and organized workers during the construction.</li><li>The defendant had no sufficient income during the initial construction period.</li></ul><p>The plaintiffs also submitted payment receipts and a valuation letter issued by the Fitche Town Housing Development Office indicating that the estimated value of the house was ETB 188,114.35.</p><h2>Defendant’s Evidence</h2><p>The defendant and her witnesses stated that:</p><ul><li>The defendant was married during the relevant period.</li><li>Her husband had construction skills and participated in building the house.</li><li>The defendant earned income through personal work activities.</li><li>An additional room/kitchen was later constructed by the defendant herself.</li><li>The plaintiffs’ claimed expenses were exaggerated and unsupported by sufficient proof.</li></ul><p>Some witnesses confirmed that the first plaintiff assisted in the construction, while others testified that the defendant and her husband also significantly contributed.</p><h2>ISSUES FOR DETERMINATION</h2><p>The Court considered the following issues:</p><ol><li>Whether the plaintiffs proved that they incurred construction expenses for the disputed house.</li><li>Whether the defendant is legally obligated to reimburse the claimed amount.</li><li>Whether the plaintiffs proved the full amount claimed.</li></ol><h2>COURT’S ANALYSIS</h2><p>After examining the pleadings, witness testimony, documentary evidence, and the applicable law, the Court makes the following findings:</p><ol><li>There is no dispute that the house and land are registered in the defendant’s name.</li><li>The evidence presented by both parties establishes that the plaintiffs contributed labor and some construction materials during the construction process.</li><li>The testimony of several witnesses confirms that the first plaintiff participated directly in the construction work and supplied certain materials.</li><li>However, the plaintiffs failed to produce complete and reliable documentary evidence proving the entirety of the claimed amount of ETB 177,674.32.</li><li>The Court also finds that the defendant and her husband participated in the construction and improvement of the property.</li><li>Therefore, while the Court accepts that the plaintiffs contributed to the construction, the Court is not satisfied that the full amount claimed has been sufficiently proven.</li></ol><h2>DECISION</h2><p>Based on the above findings and the applicable law, the Court hereby decides as follows:</p><ol><li>The Court recognizes that the plaintiffs contributed labor and certain expenses toward the construction of the disputed residential house.</li><li>However, the plaintiffs failed to sufficiently prove the entire claimed amount of ETB 177,674.32.</li><li>Accordingly, the defendant shall pay compensation in the amount of ETB __________ to the plaintiffs as reasonable reimbursement for proven contributions.</li><li>The payment shall be made within the legally prescribed execution period.</li><li>Court costs and attorney fees shall be borne by both parties proportionally as determined under the Civil Procedure Rules.</li></ol><h2>FINAL ORDER</h2><p>This judgment is rendered based on the evidence presented before the Court and the applicable laws.</p><h3>JUDGE</h3><h3>SIGNATURE</h3>',\n    'Paid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    122,\n    'civil',\n    'Court_Management_System.docx',\n    'gfffffffffff',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-27',\n    NULL,\n    'running',\n    NULL,\n    'Unpaid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    123,\n    'criminal',\n    'Court_Management_System.docx',\n    'dddddddddddddddddddddd',\n    'v',\n    '117',\n    '2026-05-14',\n    '2026-05-14',\n    '65',\n    'running',\n    NULL,\n    'Paid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    124,\n    'civil',\n    'Gabaasaa Humna Namaa Kurmaana 3ffaa bara 2018.xlsx',\n    'gfhkjk;',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-12',\n    '66',\n    'closed',\n    '<ol><li><a href=\\\"ff\\\"><strong>This presentation </strong></a>slide outlines the accomplishments of an AI-driven digital legal system designed for court records.Document Digitization: The system achieves 100% digitization of court records, entirely eliminating the need for paper files.AI Capabilities: It features a multilingual chatbot supporting English, Amharic, and Afaan Oromo, alongside an AI document analyzer that provides results in under 10 seconds.Security &amp; Testing: The platform includes 5 enforced user roles using JWT and bcrypt, 12 out of 12 test cases passed, and all 87 critical security vulnerabilities from penetration tests have been cleared.Contextual Legal AI: The system utilizes a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline to ground its AI responses in actual court rules and databases, specifically tailored to the Ethiopian context.</li></ol>',\n    'Paid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    125,\n    'criminal',\n    'Gabaasaa Humna Namaa Kurmaana 3ffaa bara 2018.xlsx',\n    'jjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjj',\n    'fw',\n    '117',\n    '2026-05-14',\n    '2026-05-14',\n    NULL,\n    'running',\n    NULL,\n    'unpaid'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `cases` (\n    `case_id`,\n    `case_type`,\n    `file_path`,\n    `description`,\n    `policeStation`,\n    `FIRNumber`,\n    `FIRDate`,\n    `registrationDate`,\n    `assigned_judge`,\n    `case_status`,\n    `judge_decision`,\n    `is_paid`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    126,\n    'civil',\n    '55119.docx',\n    'hfffffffffg',\n    '',\n    '',\n    '0000-00-00',\n    '2026-05-14',\n    '66',\n    'closed',\n    '<h2>COURT DECISION</h2><h2>GIRAAR JAARSOO DISTRICT COURT</h2><h3>FILE NO.: 126</h3><h3>DATE OF DECISION: __________</h3><h3>PLAINTIFFS</h3><ol><li>Mr. Indaalaa Charuu</li><li>Mrs. Zinnee Alamuu</li></ol><h3>DEFENDANT</h3><p>Mrs. Immabeet Indaalaa</p><h2>SUBJECT MATTER</h2><p>This case concerns a claim filed by the plaintiffs seeking reimbursement of construction expenses allegedly incurred in the construction of a residential house located in Burqaa Buukii Kebele, Fitche Town. The plaintiffs claim that they constructed the house using their own money, labor, and materials while the property was registered in the defendant’s name because she was their daughter and was underage at the time.</p><p>The plaintiffs requested payment of a total amount of ETB 177,674.32, representing expenses for construction materials, labor, roofing sheets, timber, nails, stone, mud plastering, and related construction costs.</p><p>The defendant denied the claim and argued that:</p><ol><li>The land and house are legally registered in her name.</li><li>She was married and capable of managing her own affairs during the construction period.</li><li>She and her husband contributed to the construction of the house.</li><li>The plaintiffs failed to produce sufficient documentary evidence proving the alleged expenses.</li><li>Some of the claims are barred by limitation due to the passage of time.</li></ol><h2>SUMMARY OF EVIDENCE</h2><h2>Plaintiffs’ Evidence</h2><p>The plaintiffs presented witness testimony stating that:</p><ul><li>The defendant was young when the land was obtained in her name.</li><li>The first plaintiff actively participated in the construction work.</li><li>The plaintiffs purchased roofing sheets, timber, nails, and other construction materials.</li><li>The plaintiffs provided labor and organized workers during the construction.</li><li>The defendant had no sufficient income during the initial construction period.</li></ul><p>The plaintiffs also submitted payment receipts and a valuation letter issued by the Fitche Town Housing Development Office indicating that the estimated value of the house was ETB 188,114.35.</p><h2>Defendant’s Evidence</h2><p>The defendant and her witnesses stated that:</p><ul><li>The defendant was married during the relevant period.</li><li>Her husband had construction skills and participated in building the house.</li><li>The defendant earned income through personal work activities.</li><li>An additional room/kitchen was later constructed by the defendant herself.</li><li>The plaintiffs’ claimed expenses were exaggerated and unsupported by sufficient proof.</li></ul><p>Some witnesses confirmed that the first plaintiff assisted in the construction, while others testified that the defendant and her husband also significantly contributed.</p><h2>ISSUES FOR DETERMINATION</h2><p>The Court considered the following issues:</p><ol><li>Whether the plaintiffs proved that they incurred construction expenses for the disputed house.</li><li>Whether the defendant is legally obligated to reimburse the claimed amount.</li><li>Whether the plaintiffs proved the full amount claimed.</li></ol><h2>COURT’S ANALYSIS</h2><p>After examining the pleadings, witness testimony, documentary evidence, and the applicable law, the Court makes the following findings:</p><ol><li>There is no dispute that the house and land are registered in the defendant’s name.</li><li>The evidence presented by both parties establishes that the plaintiffs contributed labor and some construction materials during the construction process.</li><li>The testimony of several witnesses confirms that the first plaintiff participated directly in the construction work and supplied certain materials.</li><li>However, the plaintiffs failed to produce complete and reliable documentary evidence proving the entirety of the claimed amount of ETB 177,674.32.</li><li>The Court also finds that the defendant and her husband participated in the construction and improvement of the property.</li><li>Therefore, while the Court accepts that the plaintiffs contributed to the construction, the Court is not satisfied that the full amount claimed has been sufficiently proven.</li></ol><h2>DECISION</h2><p>Based on the above findings and the applicable law, the Court hereby decides as follows:</p><ol><li>The Court recognizes that the plaintiffs contributed labor and certain expenses toward the construction of the disputed residential house.</li><li>However, the plaintiffs failed to sufficiently prove the entire claimed amount of ETB 177,674.32.</li><li>Accordingly, the defendant shall pay compensation in the amount of ETB __________ to the plaintiffs as reasonable reimbursement for proven contributions.</li><li>The payment shall be made within the legally prescribed execution period.</li><li>Court costs and attorney fees shall be borne by both parties proportionally as determined under the Civil Procedure Rules.</li></ol><h2>FINAL ORDER</h2><p>This judgment is rendered based on the evidence presented before the Court and the applicable laws.</p><h3>JUDGE</h3><h3>SIGNATURE</h3>',\n    'Paid'\n  );\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# DATA DUMP FOR TABLE: chat_logs\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n\nINSERT INTO\n  `chat_logs` (\n    `id`,\n    `message`,\n    `response`,\n    `language`,\n    `created_at`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    1,\n    'hello chat',\n    'ተቀበያለሁ: \\\"hello chat\\\"። የእርስዎ የኢትዮጵያ ፍርድ ቤት ረዳት ነኝ። ስለ ፍርድ ቤት ሂደቶች፣ የሕግ ቃላት እና የኢትዮጵያ ህግ ስርዓት መረጃ ልርዳችሁ እችላለሁ።',\n    'am',\n    '2026-01-14 00:31:30'\n  );\nINSERT INTO\n  `chat_logs` (\n    `id`,\n    `message`,\n    `response`,\n    `language`,\n    `created_at`\n  )\nVALUES\n  (\n    2,\n    'how are you ',\n    'ተቀበያለሁ: \\\"how are you \\\"። የእርስዎ የኢትዮጵያ ፍርድ ቤት ረዳት ነኝ። ስለ ፍርድ ቤት ሂደቶች፣ የሕግ ቃላት እና የኢትዮጵያ ህግ ስርዓት መረጃ ልርዳችሁ እችላለሁ።',\n    'am',\n    '2026-01-14 00:31:48'\n  );\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# DATA DUMP FOR TABLE: clientreferences\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# DATA DUMP FOR TABLE: clients\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n\nINSERT INTO\n  `clients` (\n    `id`,